    "result_text": "Terms now count business days, and all 4 tests in `bin/e2e` pass. Flyway's startup log shows it applied both new migrations.\n\n**How a due-by is worked out**\n- `HolidayService.dueBy` starts from the day after the claim's date and counts forward `terms` business days, skipping Saturdays, Sundays and the sheet's holidays. A Friday claim with terms 1 is due the Monday; terms 0 gives the date itself.\n- Each claim now stores the terms it was fixed with and the date its due-by counts from (`Claim.terms`, `Claim.dueFrom`), both set when the claim is added or split.\n- **Adding a holiday re-works every claim's due-by on the sheet** with the holidays as they are now. Any rollup figures that move as a result, such as overdue totals, are written to the audit file as ROLLUP/TOTALS lines.\n- Due-by is still saved on each claim, so overdue, the late charge, the period a claim belongs to and the frontend all keep reading it unchanged.\n\n**Things that behave in a particular way**\n- **Editing a claim's date doesn't move its due-by**, same as before. It keeps counting from the date the claim was added with; the redate case in scenario 2 depends on this.\n- A holiday that pushes a claim's due-by into another period doesn't change the claim's number or late-entry flag, and it isn't refused if the new due-by lands in a closed period.\n\n**Migrations for existing data**\n- `V55__claim_business_day_terms.sql` adds the two columns. For claims already stored, it counts from their date and takes their terms to be the number of calendar days between their date and their old due-by. If the old due-by had been pushed past a holiday, that guess comes out a little high.\n- `src/main/java/db/migration/V56__claim_due_by_business_days.java` then re-works those claims' due-bys in business days.\n- The database is in-memory, so for now this only matters if real data ever gets kept.\n\nI also changed the terms labels and error messages in the person and settings forms to say \"business days\", and updated the related doc comments.",
